Below is the annual "official" /dev/null/nethack announcement.

There will not be a lot of changes this year; the Trophy structure is 
staying roughly the same as last year, though we are adding a new 
Challenge Trophy (as we intend to do each year).  No, we won't tell you 
what this year's is; that's why it's a Challenge.

We forgot to take the time to deal with the Clan trophies, so that 
controversy gets to continue into yet another year.

We're currently putting the finishing touches on this year's Challenge 
patch, so folks interested in hosting this year will need to wait till 
we announce that the game server kit is ready for download.  It should 
be early this coming week.

We should be able to do pre-registrations this year, since last year's 
re-writen codebase allows for it.  This means that you can re-activate 
your player account from previous years (provided that you still have 
the same email address), rather than having to register a new account 
every year.  That system should also become available early this coming 
week.

Feel free to pass this announcement 'round and submit it to any 
appropriate venues, should you feel so inclined.

See y'all on Halloween.

-robin

#####
The NetHack season is upon us once again.

On Halloween, at midnight Pacific U.S. Time, /dev/null's Eighth Annual
NetHack Tournament (<a 
href="http://nethack.devnull.net">http://nethack.devnull.net</a>) opens. 
As with past years, the Tournament is open to anyone who'd like to play.

We're also open to anyone who'd like to volunteer to run a game server 
since, though we have a few servers lined up around North America so 
far, play can be slow across the transoceanic links.

/dev/null is a loose association of networking geeks, unincorporated and
noncommercial.  We just do this for giggles; we make no money from this 
other than what folks feel like donating.

We no longer try to describe the Trophy structure in these announcements 
anymore; it gets more complicated every year and this section had 
reached two screens of text.  A full description is available on the web 
site at <a 
href="http://nethack.devnull.net/tournament/trophies.shtml">http://nethack.devnu
ll.net/tournament/trophies.shtml</a>.

To participate in the Tournament, visit our web site <a
href="http://nethack.devnull.net">http://nethack.devnull.net</a> during 
the month and sign up for an account.  Pre-registration will be 
available in the month before the Tournament to returning players from 
previous years.

Server Requirements:

This year's Tournament will begin with servers in California and 
probably Colorado but with additional servers hopefully coming online in 
the first few days.  Since we always appreciate additional volunteer 
servers to help speed the game play up for folks not near an existing 
host, here's roughly what you need to volunteer a game server (Remember, 
before volunteering, that anyone with admin level access to a game 
server box is not allowed to compete; so don't volunteer to host if you 
want to be a contender):

	a multi-user 3.4.3 NetHack installation (thus, probably, a Unix of some 
sort; the /dev/null server is an OpenBSD system) built with the 
Tournament's custom patches

	an installed copy of our game server kit (<a
href="http://nethack.devnull.net/software">http://nethack.devnull.net/software</
a>)

	sufficient speed/disk/memory to support at least 5000 player accounts 
and 26 concurrent players (ours used to be a P133 with 32M of memory and 
5G of disk, but the motherboard died; that small a box would still work, 
though we would recommend at least 20G of disk)

	a dedicated Internet connection (ours is on a T1; cable modems and DSL 
will work fine, but it's _very_ dificult to do unless you have static IP 
numbers)

	since NetHack needs real user accounts, we'd recommend that it be a 
dedicated system (the players will need to be entered in /etc/passwd to 
play on the box)
